From b77e9dcf613d3d9e70413a2a4896a8a692586e4f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 16:40:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] sema: name promoted operand types in "invalid operands"
 diagnostics

GCC's build_binary_op applies the integer promotions before checking a
binary operator's constraints (C11 6.5.5-6.5.14) and reports the
converted types: gcc-16.2 says `short % 1.5` has 'int' and 'double',
lccc said 'short int'. On a 16-line probe 13 lines differed: char,
signed and unsigned char, unsigned short, _Bool, an unsigned-int enum
('unsigned int'), a packed enum, a narrow bit-field ('int'), a 40-bit
unsigned long bit-field (GCC keeps the bit-field type,
'long unsigned int:40') and compound assignment (`s %= 1.5`).

When binary() reports invalid operands, check_binary_operands asks
again with each operand's promoted type, through the bit-field-aware
promotion -Wformat already uses, and Operand carries the width GCC
prints for a bit-field wider than int but narrower than its declared
type. Promotion never moves an operand between the integer, floating,
pointer and record categories binary() decides on, so the verdict is
the same and valid expressions pay nothing for it.

check_operand_diagnostics.sh records the probe for gnu90, gnu17 and
gnu23 and checks the record against gcc-16 as well.
